e  different disciplines with which history  interacts to try to recover and uncover  a pattern trajectory complexity question  there you mention euro Americans a lot  you don't mention Africans or African  Americans even though you're facing  south and how does that complicate the  story the second observation observation  is that the archaeological record is  incredibly rich not always well sifted  and not well generalized from but  incredibly rich and in in as reflected  in detailed studies of site after site  after site how much of your story would  be different if you were drawing more on  the archaeological record than you are  and what might be done in a companion  piece that did that I will certainly do  a lot more with material culture in  general and I think it was well part of  that liberating moment of not having to  say everything about early American  history that led me to not write the  book I set out to write I was part of  that I make some choices and I did some  things in particular I think my choice  to stress different methods and  different chapters meant that certain  kinds of evidence didn't get stressed at  bat points when they could have been  very useful certainly understanding the  place of Native Americans in the  Imperial world you can't understand  apart from the empire of goods and the  way in which European material artifacts  in 18th century became absolutely  central to not just the economies but in  many respects the cultural identities of  Native Americans and ways that only in  many respects increased their cultural  distance from Europeans at the same time  they incorporated them in in the your  in economic world okay so I certainly  could have done a lot more with with  archeology and that is one of the things  I decided to leave out another thing I  certainly could have done a lot more  with with african-americans but one of  the striking things about that that you  know does come up over and over again  particularly I think after about the  1670s is that one small Native Americans  do get the glimmering that in in a world  that really would be run by Europeans  their situation would be much like that  of African Americans Pontiacs teacher  Nealon at really a try racial view of  the world and part of his part of the  argument of these Native American  prophets as Greg Dowd has nicely pointed  out is that the Creator put people on  different continents and gave them  different gifts and that's where the  Creator wanted them to stay and in  particular in a particular kind of  ethnocentric view of this the Creator  clearly made Native Americans first and  place them in North America made  Africans second place them in Africa and  only third as an afterthought created  Europeans and also because I suppose  because of some limitation in Europeans  they had to have a book to tell them how  to live as opposed to everyone else so  certainly I think that that has to be a  big part of the story and one of the  many things that they got left out  thank you so much I thank you all for  coming and I believe we do still have a  couple of books left for sale if anyone  would like to pick one up and ask Dan to  inscribe it good night thanks Daniel  Richter is currently a history professor  and director of the McNeil Center for  early American Studies at the University  of Pennsylvania facing east from Indian  country as published by Harvard  University Press visit hup harvard.edu  to get more information
 

